  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1135 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nThrough the Middle of Next Week: Dry and Warming\n\nAn Omega Block remains the dominant weather feature across the \nnorth central CONUS from today into the middle of next week, \nbringing a stretch of dry weather and sunnier skies. A 1026-mb \nsurface high reflection currently stretches from western Lake \nSuperior southward to the Lower Mississippi River Valley and\ngradually strengths and slides to the southeast, being centered \nover the Lower Great Lakes and Ohio River Valley for the \nbeginning of next week. \n\nReturn SSW flow sets in across the region for Sunday into \nMonday and remains in place through midweek before the ridge \nbreaks down. Despite the multiple days of southerly flow, we \nwill be slow to warm with Gulf air remaining relegated to the \ncoast. Look for highs to only rise 10-15 degrees between today \nand Wednesday given this setup.\n\n\nThursday into Saturday: Rain Returns\n\nThe first of two weather systems arrives for Thursday and \nushers a cold front through the region, though the strength of \nthis cold air advection is questionable looking at the medium \nrange ensembles with some solutions showing little for \ntemperature impacts. Rainfall impacts also are shaping up to be \nminimal with this transient system as only 20-30% of the LREF \nensembles depict rainfall amounts over 0.10\". \n\nAfter the passage of this first wave, a stout northward push of\nhigher lower tropospheric theta-e air arrives for Friday and \nthe weekend, setting the stage for a stronger Colorado low to \neject across the north-central CONUS. While the ensembles are \nsettling in on the general setup for this event, there exist \nsubstantial differences in the upper level wave structure and \nsurface low track, which of course play a large role in the \nplacement of the heaviest precipitation Friday and Saturday and \ntemperatures later in the weekend. \n\nThe signal for higher rainfall amounts has shifted more \nnortherly over the last 24 hours, but they are still close by \nand could easily shift southward at this range in the forecast. \nThe latest LREF probabilities for >0.50\" are around 50-60% and \n>1\" sits 20%. This northward probability shift also favors the \nregion to stay on the warmer side of the low. As a result, the \nprobabilities for accumulating snow on the backside of the \nsystem have trended further northward, but we could still see \nlight dusting of snow on the backside of the storm depending on \nthe strength and timing of the cold air advection.",
